In the News
Executive View
RAS inhibition crossed from proof of concept into regulatory validation.
Europe=E2=80=99s positive opinion for daraxonrasib in previously treated meta=
static pancreatic cancer establishes a commercial path for broad RAS blockade=
, while early VS-7375 data indicate selective KRAS G12D inhibitors may delive=
r higher response rates in molecularly defined populations.
Bristol Myers Squibb delivered two pivotal wins, but neither is fully interpr=
etable yet.
Admilparant validated LPA1 inhibition in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, and R=
YZ101 validated actinium-225 therapy after lutetium treatment; undisclosed ef=
fect sizes remain the principal limitation.
FDA is testing more evidence-efficient regulation.
Acceptance of a Keytruda biosimilar without a comparative Phase 3 trial and A=
nktiva=E2=80=99s label expansion through evidence extrapolation could materia=
lly reduce development requirements in selected settings.
Oral obesity treatment moved toward commercialization.
EMA advisers recommended Lilly=E2=80=99s orforglipron for weight management a=
nd type 2 diabetes, shifting competitive attention from injectable efficacy t=
oward access, manufacturing and primary-care adoption.
BioNTech=E2=80=99s pivotal miss weakens fixed-antigen mRNA cancer immunothera=
py without invalidating personalized vaccines.
BNT113 failed to improve progression-free survival when added to pembrolizuma=
b in HPV16-positive head-and-neck cancer.
Strategy Narrative
RAS therapy has reached an approval threshold=E2=80=94and competition is alre=
ady fragmenting
What changed
On October 15, EMA=E2=80=99s CHMP recommended approval of Revolution Medicine=
s=E2=80=99 daraxonrasib, branded Rasonque, for metastatic pancreatic adenocar=
cinoma after at least one prior systemic therapy. The Phase 3 results reporte=
d median overall survival of 12.4 months versus 6.7 months with chemotherapy =
and median progression-free survival of 7.2 versus 3.6 months. European Commi=
ssion authorization remains pending. (
ema.europa.eu
)
Separately, Verastem reported a 50% confirmed response rate with the 900-mg d=
ose of KRAS G12D inhibitor VS-7375 in second-line pancreatic cancer. Signals =
also emerged in colorectal and lung cancer, although the cohorts were small, =
nonrandomized and relatively immature; the first-line lung combination result=
, for example, was based on six responses among eight evaluable patients. (
investor.verastem.com
)
What it tells us
Daraxonrasib converts broad RAS inhibition from a development hypothesis into=
 a potential treatment category in one of oncology=E2=80=99s most resistant d=
iseases. VS-7375 suggests genotype-selective agents could subsequently compet=
e through higher response depth in KRAS G12D tumors. Commercially, this may p=
roduce a sequencing market rather than a single winner: broad RAS blockade ac=
ross mutations, followed by selective inhibitors optimized for common variant=
s.
What to watch next
European Commission action, FDA timing for daraxonrasib, detailed safety and =
discontinuation data, and whether VS-7375=E2=80=99s early responses translate=
 into durable randomized benefit.
BMS validated two new treatment mechanisms=E2=80=94but topline opacity limits=
 the upgrade
What changed
The 60-mg twice-daily dose of admilparant met the Phase 3 ALOFT-IPF primary e=
ndpoint by significantly reducing 52-week forced-vital-capacity decline versu=
s placebo. The 120-mg dose slowed decline but was not statistically significa=
nt. BMS reported low adverse-event discontinuation rates comparable with plac=
ebo but disclosed neither the treatment effect nor disease-progression result=
s. (
investors.bms.com
)
In the Phase 3 ACTION-1 trial, actinium-225 radiopharmaceutical RYZ101 signif=
icantly improved progression-free survival in somatostatin-receptor-positive =
gastroenteropancreatic neuroendocrine tumors that had progressed after luteti=
um-177 therapy. Effect size and detailed toxicity were again withheld. (
news.bms.com
)
What it tells us
Admilparant validates LPA1 inhibition but the inverse dose result raises ques=
tions about exposure, tolerability and statistical variability. RYZ101 is str=
ategically clearer: it supports alpha-emitting therapy after beta-emitter fai=
lure and strengthens BMS=E2=80=99s RayzeBio acquisition thesis. It also exten=
ds the radiopharmaceutical theme from last month=E2=80=99s isotope-supply tra=
nsactions into clinically differentiated sequencing.
What to watch next
Absolute FVC preservation and hepatic safety for admilparant; PFS magnitude, =
marrow toxicity and manufacturing capacity for RYZ101; and the progressive-pu=
lmonary-fibrosis ALOFT readout expected in early 2027.
FDA is selectively lowering the evidence burden
What changed
FDA accepted Formycon and Zydus=E2=80=99s FYB206 application=E2=80=94the firs=
t publicly announced Keytruda biosimilar filing=E2=80=94without a comparative=
 Phase 3 efficacy trial. The agreed pathway instead relied on analytical, fun=
ctional and pharmacokinetic comparability. (
formycon.com
)
FDA also expanded Anktiva plus BCG to BCG-unresponsive, high-grade papillary-=
only non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer without a direct efficacy trial in th=
at population, relying on prior carcinoma-in-situ evidence and scientific sim=
ilarities between the tumor forms. On the same day, the agency launched a con=
sultative Rare Disease Framework Initiative intended to tailor evidence expec=
tations to disease, population and product characteristics; it is not yet gui=
dance or a binding policy. (
fda.gov
)
What it tells us
These actions indicate a more explicit willingness to substitute strong analy=
tical evidence, mechanistic similarity or adjacent clinical data for duplicat=
ive trials. The immediate commercial consequence is potentially faster, cheap=
er biosimilar development. The broader implication is that sponsors with well=
-characterized biology and defensible external evidence may gain more flexibl=
e development pathways=E2=80=94although FDA states that statutory safety and =
effectiveness standards remain unchanged.
Oral GLP-1 competition has entered the regulatory phase
What changed
EMA=E2=80=99s CHMP recommended Lilly=E2=80=99s oral GLP-1 receptor agonist or=
forglipron, under the proposed brand Foundayo, for adult weight management an=
d type 2 diabetes. European Commission approval remains required. (
ema.europa.eu
)
What it tells us
The obesity market is moving beyond maximum injectable weight loss toward for=
mat, supply and reach. An effective oral small molecule could expand treatmen=
t in primary care and reduce dependence on injectable-peptide manufacturing. =
Its ultimate positioning will depend on efficacy, gastrointestinal tolerabili=
ty, adherence and pricing relative to semaglutide, tirzepatide and retatrutid=
e=E2=80=94not oral administration alone.
Therapeutic & Clinical Signals
BioNTech=E2=80=99s BNT113 result narrows the mRNA cancer-vaccine thesis.
Adding a fixed HPV16 E6/E7 mRNA immunotherapy to pembrolizumab did not signif=
icantly improve progression-free survival in first-line PD-L1-positive head-a=
nd-neck cancer. Overall survival remains a co-primary endpoint and can still =
make the trial formally positive, but the absence of any disclosed PFS effect=
 size lowers confidence. The result should not be directly extrapolated to in=
dividualized neoantigen vaccines, which use a different antigen-selection str=
ategy. (
biontech.com
)
Obuprazine generated an unusually large schizophrenia signal that now require=
s geographic replication.
In 363 Chinese adults with acute schizophrenia, all three doses significantly=
 improved PANSS scores; the lowest dose produced a 16.7-point placebo-adjuste=
d improvement and numerically exceeded aripiprazole. The six-week inpatient s=
tudy does not yet establish long-term metabolic, movement-disorder or adheren=
ce differentiation. (
tortugasneuro.com
)
Company & Capital Signals
Recursion produced a partnered-platform validation but financed it with dilut=
ion.
Sanofi optioned the collaboration=E2=80=99s first development candidate, trig=
gering a $10 million milestone, while Recursion simultaneously raised $200 mi=
llion. The option is evidence that the platform can generate externally selec=
ted assets; it is not yet human validation, with Phase 1 planned for 2027. (
recursion.gcs-web.com
)
OMass raised $310 million to advance its MC2 receptor antagonist into Phase 2=
 for congenital adrenal hyperplasia and ACTH-dependent Cushing=E2=80=99s synd=
rome.
The unusually large Series C reflects continued capital availability for diff=
erentiated endocrine mechanisms, but clinical proof remains ahead. (
globenewswire.com
)
What I=E2=80=99m Watching Next
Daraxonrasib=E2=80=99s U.S. regulatory path and full Phase 3 safety profile.
Quantitative admilparant and RYZ101 Phase 3 results.
Whether FDA formalizes its evidence-extrapolation approach beyond isolated ca=
ses.
BNT113 overall survival and next week=E2=80=99s individualized mRNA-vaccine d=
ata.
Orforglipron=E2=80=99s final European authorization and commercial positionin=
g.
Bottom Line
This week rewarded mechanisms that crossed into clinically or regulatorily us=
able form: broad RAS inhibition, post-lutetium actinium therapy, oral GLP-1 t=
reatment and streamlined biosimilar development. It also reinforced the evide=
ntiary divide between promise and adoption. Strong topline claims without eff=
ect sizes=E2=80=94admilparant, RYZ101 and BNT113=E2=80=94remain strategically=
 incomplete until durability, safety and absolute benefit are disclosed.
Recent Earnings Highlights
Johnson & Johnson (
JNJ
)
+1.0% Last 3m
Johnson & Johnson is the world's largest and most diverse healthcare firm. It=
 has two divisions: innovative medicine and medtech. These now represent all =
of the company's sales following the divestment of the consumer business, Ken=
vue, in 2023. After restructurings in 2023-24, the drug division focuses on t=
hree main therapeutic areas: immunology, oncology, and neurology. Geographica=
lly, just over half of total revenue is generated in the United States.
Reported:
October 13, 2026 =C2=B7
Google Finance earnings page
Earnings Call Summary
Johnson & Johnson reported Q3 2026 operational sales growth of 6% to over $25=
 billion, driven by 7.9% operational growth in Innovative Medicine with stron=
g oncology performance (+17%). The company is on track to exceed $100 billion=
 in annual revenue for the first time in its 140-year history and expects dou=
ble-digit growth by 2030.
At a Glance
J&J Beats Revenue and EPS Estimates:
Johnson & Johnson reported Q3 2026 adjusted EPS of $0.09 and reported revenue=
 of $25,399,000,000, slightly outpacing the estimated adjusted EPS of 2.522 a=
nd estimated revenue of 25,382,141,400.
Innovative Medicine Outpaces Slowing MedTech:
The Innovative Medicine segment delivered robust operational growth of 7.9% d=
riven by double-digit oncology sales, whereas the MedTech segment disappointe=
d with a soft 2.5% growth rate that fell short of internal standards.
Full-Year 2026 Guidance Revised Upward:
Management raised its full-year 2026 operational sales growth forecast to a r=
ange of 6.7% to 7.1% and increased full-year adjusted EPS guidance to between=
 $11.05 and $11.15.
Litigation and M&A Impact Profits:
Reported GAAP net earnings dropped sharply due to approximately $7 billion in=
 special charges stemming from a proposed talc litigation resolution and rece=
nt acquisitions like Firefly Bio.
Orthopedics Separation Teased for 2027:
Management indicated they are leaning toward spinning off the DePuy Synthes o=
rthopedics business unit by mid-2027 as an optimal strategic initiative to un=
lock value.
